.Dd October 2, 2026
.Dt PG_LASWELL_TIMESCALEDB 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pg_laswell_timescaledb
.Nd TimescaleDB support in pg_laswell: hypertables, the columnstore, policies and continuous aggregates
.Sh DESCRIPTION
TimescaleDB support is a
.Em build-time module ,
like Citus support
.Pq Xr pg_laswell_citus 7 .
It adds the kinds below, and it changes how core plans an index on a hypertable
\(em which, without it, core plans wrongly: a hypertable's parent holds no rows,
so core read it as tiny and built indexes on it with a lock on every chunk, or
with
.Ic CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY ,
which TimescaleDB refuses.
.Pp
A binary that carries the module says so:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ pg_laswell_mcp --version
0.1.3
modules: citus,pg_cron,pgvector,timescaledb
.Ed
.Pp
.Sy Versions and editions.
Measured on TimescaleDB 2.30.2 with PostgreSQL 18, in both editions, and on
2.28.3 with PostgreSQL 15, the oldest TimescaleDB line on the oldest PostgreSQL
this project supports.
Which functions exist is read from the server, not inferred from a version.
.Pp
TimescaleDB ships in two editions, and the same binary meets both.
The
.Em Apache
build \(em what PGDG and Debian package as
.Li postgresql-NN-timescaledb
.Pq Li +dfsg ,
and the
.Li -oss
images \(em has hypertables and chunks.
The
.Em Timescale License
build \(em Timescale's own packages and images \(em adds the columnstore,
continuous aggregates and policies.
The module reads
.Va timescaledb.license
and refuses the second kind of intent on the first edition, before anything
runs, quoting
.Qq functionality not supported under the current \&"apache\&" license .
.Pp
TimescaleDB must be preloaded and created in the database before its kinds can
be planned: a specification cannot both create the extension and use it.
.Sh INTENT KINDS
.\" BEGIN TIMESCALEDB KIND LIST -- checked by tools/check-manual.py against
.\" cpp/src/modules/timescaledb/kinds.inc.
Every kind accepts an optional
.Va comment ,
a note for whoever reads the specification.
Intervals are written as one or more
.Ql <number> <unit>
pairs \(em year, month, week, day, hour, minute, second \(em and sent to
PostgreSQL as written.
They are compared as PostgreSQL keeps them, as months, days and seconds, so
.Ql 1 week
and
.Ql 7 days
are the same and
.Ql 7 days
and
.Ql 168 hours
are not.
